BestFirstSearch Test Report

This report describes the current host-native BestFirstSearch test. It uses
the current production API and the real SafePowerLimitCalculator and
LoadFilter inputs used by the central optimization path.

1. Run command

From the project root:

g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti \
  -Ilib/BestFirstSearch -Ilib/LoadManager -Ilib/LoadManager/Central \
  -Ilib/BatteryManager -Ilib/FirmwareManager \
  lib/BestFirstSearch/BestFirstSearch.cpp \
  lib/LoadManager/Load.cpp \
  lib/LoadManager/Central/LoadFilter.cpp \
  lib/LoadManager/Central/LoadScheduleEvaluator.cpp \
  lib/BatteryManager/PowerManager.cpp \
  lib/FirmwareManager/CurrentTimeProvider.cpp \
  test/BestFirstSearch/test_best_first_search.cpp \
  -o /tmp/test_best_first_search && /tmp/test_best_first_search

2. Test groups

Candidate scoring and ordering
  Validates weights, score terms, selection, planned power and remaining power.

Constraint rejection reasons
  Checks LOW_BATTERY, POWER_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, BATTERY_CURRENT_LIMIT and
  MAIN_LIMIT_EXCEEDED.

Sequential allocation
  Confirms that a candidate that fits is selected and a later candidate that
  exceeds the remaining power is rejected.

Static feasibility
  Directly checks the production feasibility function for valid and invalid
  electrical snapshots.

Controlled exact optimality
  Enumerates every subset of four loads and confirms that Best-First selects
  the exact best subset for that deliberately small controlled case.

3. Real four-node battery scenario

The test builds one fixed installation with four nodes and four loads per
node, for 16 loads total:

Node A: Fridge, Lights, TV, Kettle
Node B: Lights, AC, Garage, Heater
Node C: Pump, Washer, Pool, Lights
Node D: Router, Fans, Charger, EV

Each load uses a real MAC address plus relay pin. Each node has one FIXED_ON
load and three AUTO candidates, including AUTO_ON, AUTO_OFF and schedule
penalty examples.

The complete battery sweep executes 12 scenarios:

Battery capacity: 20 Ah, 100 Ah and 1,000 Ah
State of charge: 25%, 50%, 75% and 100%

For every scenario the test runs:

1. SafePowerLimitCalculator
2. LoadFilter classification
3. BestFirstSearch planning
4. Safe Auto-load allocation checks
5. Coordinated versus uncoordinated runtime calculations

The scenario checks that all 16 loads are accepted, all 13 Auto loads reach
Best-First, the search completes, and admitted Auto power stays within the
power left after Fixed ON loads. It also checks that increasing SoC or battery
capacity does not reduce coordinated load power.

4. Latest result

Passed checks: 157
Failed checks: 0
Overall result: PASS

The scenario output includes the battery capacity, SoC, safe available power,
uncoordinated demand, coordinated demand and the two runtime estimates for
each of the 12 cases.

5. Scope limitation

The 12 real battery scenarios validate constraint handling, multi-node load
classification, coordinated allocation and runtime improvement. They do not
claim exact global optimality for all 13 Auto loads. Exact brute-force
optimality is currently checked only in the separate four-load controlled
case because exhaustive enumeration grows exponentially with candidate count.

Hardware-only behavior remains outside this host test, including INA219
calibration, relay operation, ESP-NOW communication, ESP32 timing and
long-term battery discharge accuracy.

6. Separate simulation tests and CSV artifacts

The simulations are intentionally separate files:

test_exact_optimality.cpp
  Ten-load exhaustive subset comparison. Writes
  test/report/csv/exact_optimality.csv.

test_simulation_16_loads.cpp
  Four real nodes with four named loads per node, twelve battery scenarios.
  Writes real_16_load_inventory.csv and real_16_load_scenarios.csv.

test_simulation_scalability.cpp
  Thirty-two, sixty-four and 128-load simulations, with 100 and 1,000
  repetitions at 100 Ah and 1,000 Ah. Writes scalability_summary.csv.

test_battery_cycle.cpp
  Twenty-four hourly battery-only optimization cycles with changing SoC.
  Writes battery_cycle.csv with the SoC and energy change at
  every hour, plus battery_savings_summary.csv with one managed-versus-
  unmanaged savings row for every capacity and starting-SoC combination.
  Solar generation is deliberately excluded because the current firmware
  repository has no PV input, solar controller, or solar charger model.

The CSV files are generated test artifacts and are kept under version control
in test/report/csv. Run each test through the project runner:

./run_cpp_test.sh test/BestFirstSearch/test_exact_optimality.cpp
./run_cpp_test.sh test/BestFirstSearch/test_simulation_16_loads.cpp
./run_cpp_test.sh test/BestFirstSearch/test_simulation_scalability.cpp
./run_cpp_test.sh test/BestFirstSearch/test_battery_cycle.cpp
